exec(open('/home/claude/work/gita/gc.py').read())
V=[
X(1,'WHICH ONE IS BETTER?',
 S('Arjuna holding two signs: renounce and act','A','scale','WHICH?'),
 'Arjuna said: You praise the renunciation of action, Kṛṣṇa, and then yoga (action). Tell me for certain which one of these two is better.',
 [('sannyāsam karmaṇām','<b>renunciation of actions</b>'),('punaḥ yogam ca śaṃsasi','<b>and again you praise yoga</b>'),('yat śreyaḥ etayoḥ ekam','<b>which one of these is better</b>'),('tat me brūhi su-niścitam','<b>tell me that, clearly decided</b>')],
 'Chapter 4 praised both giving up action (in knowledge) and doing it (in yoga). Arjuna asks again for one clear answer.',
 'This is Arjuna’s second request for “one thing” (compare 3.2). Commentators note he still thinks in either-or terms; Kṛṣṇa will show the two are not truly opposed.',
 'Many life choices look like either-or. Often a wiser view finds a way that includes both.',
 'Renounce or act: <b>which is better?</b>'),
X(2,'BOTH LEAD TO FREEDOM',
 S('Two paths meeting at the same light','K','mountain','BOTH FREE'),
 'The Lord said: Renunciation and the yoga of action both lead to the highest good. But of the two, the yoga of action is better than the renunciation of action.',
 [('sannyāsaḥ karma-yogaḥ ca','<b>renunciation and karma yoga</b>'),('niḥśreyasa-karau ubhau','<b>both bring the highest good</b>'),('tayoḥ tu','<b>but of the two</b>'),('karma-sannyāsāt','<b>than renouncing action</b>'),('karma-yogaḥ viśiṣyate','<b>karma yoga is better</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa’s answer: both lead to freedom, but karma yoga is better for Arjuna.',
 'Śaṅkara explains “better” as easier and more suitable for one not yet ready for full renunciation. Rāmānuja reads karma yoga as superior in itself, being free of risk. Both agree it is the right path for Arjuna now.',
 'For most of us, doing our duties in the right spirit is the surer path than leaving them.',
 'Both free; <b>acting is better for most</b>.'),
X(3,'THE TRUE RENUNCIANT',
 S('A householder at work who hates nothing and craves nothing, glowing','P','light','NITYA-SANNYĀSĪ'),
 '“Know him as a constant renunciant who neither hates nor desires. Free from the pairs of opposites, mighty-armed one, he is easily freed from bondage.”',
 [('jñeyaḥ saḥ nitya-sannyāsī','<b>he is to be known as ever a renunciant</b>'),('yaḥ na dveṣṭi na kāṅkṣati','<b>who neither hates nor desires</b>'),('nirdvandvaḥ hi','<b>free of opposites</b>'),('sukham bandhāt pramucyate','<b>is easily freed from bondage</b>')],
 'Renunciation is inside: one who neither hates nor craves is a renunciant, whatever he wears.',
 'This redefines <i>sannyāsa</i>. It is not leaving home but leaving likes and dislikes. Commentators say a karma yogi with this inner freedom is already a sannyāsī.',
 'You don’t need to wear orange. Drop hatred and craving, and you have renounced what matters.',
 'True renunciation <b>is inner</b>.'),
X(4,'ONLY CHILDREN SEE THEM AS DIFFERENT',
 S('Two paths that are really one, a child confused','K','book','SAME GOAL'),
 '“Children, not the wise, speak of Sāṅkhya and yoga as different. One truly established in either gains the fruit of both.”',
 [('sāṅkhya-yogau pṛthak','<b>knowledge and yoga as separate</b>'),('bālāḥ pravadanti','<b>children say</b>'),('na paṇḍitāḥ','<b>not the wise</b>'),('ekam api āsthitaḥ samyak','<b>established well in even one</b>'),('ubhayoḥ vindate phalam','<b>gains the fruit of both</b>')],
 'Knowledge and action are not rivals. Mastery of one brings the fruit of both.',
 '<i>Bāla</i>, child, means the immature in understanding. Commentators note that the Gītā repeatedly reconciles paths that people set against each other.',
 'Don’t get stuck arguing which path is best. Walk one sincerely.',
 'Knowledge and action <b>lead to one goal</b>.'),
X(5,'ONE WHO SEES TRULY',
 S('A person seeing two paths as one','Y','light','THEY ARE ONE'),
 '“The state reached by the followers of Sāṅkhya is reached by the yogis too. One who sees Sāṅkhya and yoga as one truly sees.”',
 [('yat sāṅkhyaiḥ prāpyate sthānam','<b>the state reached by Sāṅkhyas</b>'),('tat yogaiḥ api gamyate','<b>is reached by yogis too</b>'),('ekam sāṅkhyam ca yogam ca','<b>knowledge and yoga as one</b>'),('yaḥ paśyati saḥ paśyati','<b>who sees thus, sees</b>')],
 'The same goal for both.',
 '“He who sees, sees”: a typical Gītā phrase for true vision (also 13.27). Commentators agree the goal is the same freedom.',
 'Different roads, same destination. That vision brings peace between paths.',
 'Who sees them as one, <b>truly sees</b>.'),
X(6,'HARD WITHOUT YOGA',
 S('A man struggling to renounce, a yogi walking easily ahead','Y','mountain','EASIER WITH YOGA'),
 '“But renunciation is hard to attain without yoga, mighty-armed one. The sage joined in yoga soon reaches Brahman.”',
 [('sannyāsaḥ tu','<b>but renunciation</b>'),('duḥkham āptum ayogataḥ','<b>is hard to attain without yoga</b>'),('yoga-yuktaḥ muniḥ','<b>the sage joined in yoga</b>'),('brahma na cireṇa adhigacchati','<b>soon reaches Brahman</b>')],
 'Trying to renounce without first practising karma yoga is painful.',
 'Commentators: karma yoga purifies the mind; with a pure mind, renunciation becomes natural and quick. Without it, renunciation is forced and fragile.',
 'Prepare before you leap. Inner work makes outer change easier.',
 'Yoga first; <b>renunciation follows</b>.'),
X(7,'THE SELF OF ALL BEINGS',
 S('A pure-hearted yogi seeing his own self in all beings','Y','family','SAME SELF'),
 '“Joined in yoga, pure in heart, master of himself and his senses, whose self has become the self of all beings: even acting, he is not stained.”',
 [('yoga-yuktaḥ viśuddha-ātmā','<b>in yoga, pure in heart</b>'),('vijita-ātmā jita-indriyaḥ','<b>self-mastered, senses conquered</b>'),('sarva-bhūta-ātma-bhūta-ātmā','<b>whose self is the self of all beings</b>'),('kurvan api na lipyate','<b>even acting, is not stained</b>')],
 'Such a person sees himself in all, and his actions do not stain him.',
 'One who feels the self of all beings as his own cannot act selfishly. Commentators say this is why his actions do not bind.',
 'When we feel others as ourselves, right action becomes natural.',
 'One with all, <b>unstained by action</b>.'),
X(8,'“I DO NOTHING AT ALL”',
 S('A sage seeing, hearing, walking, but thinking: the senses are acting','Y','mind','NAIVA KIÑCIT'),
 '“‘I do nothing at all,’ thinks the yogi who knows the truth: while seeing, hearing, touching, smelling, eating, walking, sleeping, breathing,”',
 [('na eva kiñcit karomi','<b>I do nothing at all</b>'),('iti yuktaḥ manyeta','<b>thus the yogi should think</b>'),('tattva-vit','<b>knower of truth</b>'),('paśyan śṛṇvan spṛśan jighran','<b>seeing, hearing, touching, smelling</b>'),('aśnan gacchan svapan śvasan','<b>eating, walking, sleeping, breathing</b>')],
 'The knower sees all activity as the senses at work, not the Self.',
 'This continues into 5.9. It deepens 3.27: the guṇas act; the Self witnesses. Commentators stress it is a vision of truth, not a technique to dodge responsibility.',
 'Watch yourself eating or walking as if from a little distance. It brings calm.',
 'The Self <b>is the witness</b>.'),
X(9,'THE SENSES AMONG THEIR OBJECTS',
 S('Eyes opening and closing, the self just watching','Y','light','JUST SENSES'),
 '“…speaking, letting go, grasping, opening and closing the eyes, he holds that the senses are moving among their objects.”',
 [('pralapan visṛjan gṛhṇan','<b>speaking, releasing, grasping</b>'),('unmiṣan nimiṣan api','<b>opening and closing the eyes</b>'),('indriyāṇi indriya-artheṣu','<b>the senses among their objects</b>'),('vartante iti dhārayan','<b>holding that they move</b>')],
 'Even blinking is just the senses at work.',
 'The list covers every activity, voluntary and involuntary. Commentators say the point is complete detachment: the Self is never the doer.',
 'Even ordinary moments can be seen with this calm, witnessing attention.',
 'Senses move <b>among objects</b>.'),
X(10,'LIKE A LOTUS LEAF',
 S('A lotus leaf with water drops rolling off','','lotus','PADMA-PATRA'),
 '“One who acts, offering actions to Brahman and giving up attachment, is not stained by sin, as a lotus leaf is not wetted by water.”',
 [('brahmaṇi ādhāya karmāṇi','<b>offering actions to Brahman</b>'),('saṅgam tyaktvā karoti yaḥ','<b>who acts giving up attachment</b>'),('lipyate na saḥ pāpena','<b>is not stained by sin</b>'),('padma-patram iva ambhasā','<b>like a lotus leaf by water</b>')],
 'The most beautiful image of detachment.',
 'The lotus grows in mud and water, yet its leaf stays dry. Commentators love this image: live in the world, unwetted by it. It is used across Indian art and teaching.',
 'Live fully in the world; don’t let it soak in.',
 'In the water, <b>not wet by it</b>.'),
X(11,'WORKING TO PURIFY',
 S('A yogi working with body, mind and intellect, a stream of purity flowing','Y','light','ĀTMA-ŚUDDHI'),
 '“With body, mind, intellect, and even the senses alone, yogis act, giving up attachment, for self-purification.”',
 [('kāyena manasā buddhyā','<b>with body, mind, intellect</b>'),('kevalaiḥ indriyaiḥ api','<b>even with the senses alone</b>'),('yoginaḥ karma kurvanti','<b>yogis act</b>'),('saṅgam tyaktvā','<b>giving up attachment</b>'),('ātma-śuddhaye','<b>for self-purification</b>')],
 'Why do yogis act at all? To purify themselves.',
 'Commentators say karma yoga is a cleansing process: each act done without attachment wears away selfishness. Śaṅkara stresses this as the path to knowledge.',
 'Think of daily duties as a gym for the soul. Each one done well strengthens you.',
 'Yogis act <b>to purify</b>.'),
X(12,'PEACE OR BONDAGE',
 S('Two workers: one peaceful, the other chained to rewards','P','chain','PEACE · CHAINS'),
 '“The yogi, giving up the fruit of action, reaches lasting peace. The one not in yoga, attached to fruit, is bound by desire.”',
 [('yuktaḥ karma-phalam tyaktvā','<b>the yogi, giving up fruit</b>'),('śāntim āpnoti naiṣṭhikīm','<b>reaches lasting peace</b>'),('ayuktaḥ kāma-kāreṇa','<b>the unyoked, by desire</b>'),('phale saktaḥ nibadhyate','<b>attached to fruit, is bound</b>')],
 'Same work; two outcomes: peace, or chains.',
 'Commentators note the contrast is entirely inner. <i>Naiṣṭhikī</i> śānti is peace that lasts, rooted in steadiness.',
 'Work for peace, not for rewards. Peace lasts; rewards pass.',
 'Let go of fruit: <b>lasting peace</b>.'),
X(13,'THE CITY OF NINE GATES',
 S('A small city with nine gates, a happy ruler resting inside','Y','light','9 GATES'),
 '“Renouncing all actions with the mind, the embodied one rests happily, self-controlled, in the city of nine gates, neither acting nor causing action.”',
 [('sarva-karmāṇi manasā','<b>all actions with the mind</b>'),('sannyasya āste sukham','<b>renouncing, sits happily</b>'),('vaśī','<b>self-controlled</b>'),('nava-dvāre pure dehī','<b>the embodied one in the city of nine gates</b>'),('na eva kurvan na kārayan','<b>neither doing nor causing</b>')],
 'The body as a city with nine gates: two eyes, two ears, two nostrils, mouth, and two lower openings.',
 'The image comes from the Upaniṣads (Kaṭha, Śvetāśvatara). The Self lives in the city like a resident, not as a labourer. Commentators note “with the mind”: the renunciation is mental.',
 'Your body is your city. Be its calm resident, not its slave.',
 'The Self rests in <b>the city of nine gates</b>.'),
X(14,'THE LORD DOES NOT MAKE DOERSHIP',
 S('Krishna stepping back while nature turns its own wheel','K','wheel','SVABHĀVA'),
 '“The Lord does not create doership or actions for the world, nor the link of action and fruit. It is nature (svabhāva) that works.”',
 [('na kartṛtvam na karmāṇi','<b>not doership nor actions</b>'),('lokasya sṛjati prabhuḥ','<b>does the Lord create for the world</b>'),('na karma-phala-saṃyogam','<b>nor the union of action and fruit</b>'),('svabhāvaḥ tu pravartate','<b>but nature works</b>')],
 'God does not push us into actions; our own nature does.',
 'This is subtle. Śaṅkara says the Self (Lord) is actionless; nature (māyā) acts. Others say God sets up the law of karma, but our choices and nature drive it. The verse frees God from blame for our deeds.',
 'Our habits and nature shape what we do. Blaming God or fate misses where change can happen.',
 'Our nature acts, <b>not the Lord</b>.'),
X(15,'KNOWLEDGE COVERED BY IGNORANCE',
 S('A light covered by a dark cloth, confused people below','','light','COVERED',SKY,'<rect x="120" y="60" width="80" height="80" fill="#3a3a3a" opacity=".6"/>'),
 '“The all-pervading one takes on no one’s sin or merit. Knowledge is covered by ignorance; by that, beings are deluded.”',
 [('na ādatte kasyacit pāpam','<b>takes no one’s sin</b>'),('na ca eva sukṛtam vibhuḥ','<b>nor merit, the all-pervading</b>'),('ajñānena āvṛtam jñānam','<b>knowledge covered by ignorance</b>'),('tena muhyanti jantavaḥ','<b>by that beings are deluded</b>')],
 'The Lord is untouched by our good and bad deeds. Our trouble is ignorance covering knowledge.',
 'Commentators: the root problem is not sin but ignorance (<i>ajñāna</i>). Remove the covering and the light was always there.',
 'Often what we need is not more effort but more seeing. Light was there all along.',
 'Ignorance <b>covers the light</b>.'),
X(16,'LIKE THE SUN',
 S('A sun rising and lighting up everything','','sun','LIKE THE SUN'),
 '“But for those whose ignorance is destroyed by knowledge of the Self, that knowledge, like the sun, reveals the Supreme.”',
 [('jñānena tu tat ajñānam','<b>but by knowledge that ignorance</b>'),('yeṣām nāśitam ātmanaḥ','<b>of those, destroyed, of the Self</b>'),('teṣām āditya-vat jñānam','<b>for them, like the sun, knowledge</b>'),('prakāśayati tat param','<b>reveals the Supreme</b>')],
 'Knowledge shines like the sun.',
 'The sun does not create the world; it reveals it. Likewise, knowledge does not create the Self; it reveals it. Commentators love the image.',
 'Understanding doesn’t make reality; it shows it. Seek light, not new things.',
 'Knowledge shines <b>like the sun</b>.'),
X(17,'NO RETURN',
 S('A seeker walking into a great light, no path back','Y','light','APUNAR-ĀVṚTTI'),
 '“Those whose understanding is in That, whose self is That, who are established in That, who have That as the supreme goal, their sins washed away by knowledge, go to the state of no return.”',
 [('tat-buddhayaḥ tat-ātmānaḥ','<b>mind in That, self in That</b>'),('tat-niṣṭhāḥ tat-parāyaṇāḥ','<b>established in That, devoted to That</b>'),('gacchanti apunar-āvṛttim','<b>go to non-return</b>'),('jñāna-nirdhūta-kalmaṣāḥ','<b>sins shaken off by knowledge</b>')],
 'Total absorption in the Highest leads to freedom from rebirth.',
 '“That” (<i>tat</i>) is Brahman, as in “tat tvam asi”. The four-fold repetition shows complete dedication: thought, self, foundation, goal. Commentators say such a one does not return to birth.',
 'Let one aim gather thought, identity and purpose. That focus itself frees.',
 'Absorbed in That: <b>no more return</b>.'),
X(18,'THE SAME EYE',
 S('A wise person looking equally at a learned man, a cow, an elephant, a dog and an outcaste','Y','cow','SAMA-DARŚINAḤ'),
 '“The wise see with an equal eye a learned and humble Brahmin, a cow, an elephant, a dog and an outcaste.”',
 [('vidyā-vinaya-sampanne','<b>endowed with learning and humility</b>'),('brāhmaṇe gavi hastini','<b>in a Brahmin, cow, elephant</b>'),('śuni ca eva śvapāke ca','<b>in a dog and a dog-eater</b>'),('paṇḍitāḥ sama-darśinaḥ','<b>the wise see equally</b>')],
 'A famous verse of equal vision: the same Self in all beings, whatever their form or status.',
 '<i>Śvapāka</i>, one who cooks dogs, was the lowest outcaste. The Gītā places him beside the learned Brahmin. Commentators agree: the wise see the same Self, though they still act appropriately with each. Many modern reformers quoted this verse against caste prejudice.',
 'Look past status and form. The same life looks out from every face.',
 'The wise see <b>the same Self in all</b>.'),
X(19,'EVENNESS IS BRAHMAN',
 S('A level line shining, a person standing on it at peace','Y','scale','SAMA = BRAHMA'),
 '“Even here, the world is conquered by those whose minds are established in evenness. Brahman is flawless and even; so they are established in Brahman.”',
 [('iha eva taiḥ jitaḥ sargaḥ','<b>even here birth is conquered by them</b>'),('yeṣām sāmye sthitam manaḥ','<b>whose mind is in evenness</b>'),('nirdoṣam hi samam brahma','<b>Brahman is flawless and even</b>'),('tasmāt brahmaṇi te sthitāḥ','<b>so they are in Brahman</b>')],
 'Evenness of mind is itself living in Brahman, even in this life.',
 '“Even here”: freedom is not only after death. Commentators call this <i>jīvanmukti</i>, liberation while living.',
 'An even mind is a little heaven, here and now.',
 'Evenness here <b>is living in Brahman</b>.'),
X(20,'NEITHER THRILLED NOR SHAKEN',
 S('A person receiving a gift and a loss with the same calm','Y','scale','STHIRA-BUDDHI'),
 '“Let him not rejoice on getting what is pleasant, nor be shaken on getting what is unpleasant. Steady of mind, undeluded, the knower of Brahman is established in Brahman.”',
 [('na prahṛṣyet priyam prāpya','<b>not elated on getting the pleasant</b>'),('na udvijet prāpya ca apriyam','<b>not shaken by the unpleasant</b>'),('sthira-buddhiḥ asammūḍhaḥ','<b>steady, undeluded</b>'),('brahma-vit brahmaṇi sthitaḥ','<b>knower of Brahman, in Brahman</b>')],
 'The marks of one who knows Brahman.',
 'This repeats the evenness of 2.57. Commentators note: no wild highs, no crushing lows; a steady mind, not a cold one.',
 'Steady through ups and downs: that is strength.',
 'Steady in pleasant and unpleasant: <b>rooted in Brahman</b>.'),
X(21,'JOY WITHIN',
 S('A person discovering a spring of joy inside','Y','heart','INNER JOY'),
 '“Unattached to outer contacts, he finds the joy that is in the Self. Joined by yoga to Brahman, he enjoys imperishable happiness.”',
 [('bāhya-sparśeṣu asakta-ātmā','<b>unattached to outer contacts</b>'),('vindati ātmani yat sukham','<b>finds the joy that is in the Self</b>'),('saḥ brahma-yoga-yukta-ātmā','<b>joined by yoga to Brahman</b>'),('sukham akṣayam aśnute','<b>enjoys imperishable joy</b>')],
 'A joy that does not depend on outer things.',
 'Commentators say once this inner joy is tasted, outer pleasures lose their pull (compare 2.59).',
 'Find what gives you quiet joy without needing anything. Return to it often.',
 'An <b>imperishable joy within</b>.'),
X(22,'PLEASURES ARE WOMBS OF PAIN',
 S('A tempting fruit with a worm inside','P','food','DUḤKHA-YONI'),
 '“Pleasures born of contact are sources of pain; they have a beginning and an end, son of Kuntī. The wise do not delight in them.”',
 [('ye hi saṃsparśa-jāḥ bhogāḥ','<b>pleasures born of contact</b>'),('duḥkha-yonayaḥ eva te','<b>are wombs of pain</b>'),('ādi-antavantaḥ','<b>having a beginning and end</b>'),('na teṣu ramate budhaḥ','<b>the wise do not delight in them</b>')],
 'Sense pleasures carry pain within them: they end, and leave craving.',
 '<i>Duḥkha-yoni</i>, womb of sorrow: pain is born from them, through loss, craving and boredom. This is not a rejection of life but a realistic view.',
 'Enjoy, but don’t build your happiness on what must end.',
 'Contact-pleasures <b>end in pain</b>.'),
X(23,'BEFORE LEAVING THE BODY',
 S('A person calmly withstanding a storm of desire and anger','Y','storm','WITHSTAND'),
 '“One who can withstand, here, before leaving the body, the force born of desire and anger, is a yogi; he is a happy man.”',
 [('śaknoti iha eva yaḥ soḍhum','<b>who can withstand even here</b>'),('prāk śarīra-vimokṣaṇāt','<b>before release from the body</b>'),('kāma-krodha-udbhavam vegam','<b>the surge of desire and anger</b>'),('saḥ yuktaḥ saḥ sukhī naraḥ','<b>he is a yogi, a happy man</b>')],
 'The test of a yogi: holding steady in the surge of desire and anger, in this life.',
 '<i>Vega</i>, surge or rush, like a flood. Commentators note “before death”: the chance is now.',
 'When anger surges, wait. The wave passes, and you are freer for not riding it.',
 'Withstand the surge: <b>be happy</b>.'),
X(24,'LIGHT WITHIN',
 S('A person glowing from within','Y','light','ANTAR-JYOTIḤ'),
 '“One whose happiness is within, whose delight is within, whose light is within: that yogi, becoming Brahman, reaches the peace of Brahman.”',
 [('yaḥ antaḥ-sukhaḥ','<b>whose happiness is within</b>'),('antar-ārāmaḥ','<b>whose delight is within</b>'),('tathā antar-jyotiḥ','<b>whose light is within</b>'),('saḥ yogī brahma-bhūtaḥ','<b>that yogi, become Brahman</b>'),('brahma-nirvāṇam adhigacchati','<b>reaches the peace of Brahman</b>')],
 'Happiness, joy and light all within.',
 'Three “withins”: <i>antaḥ-sukha, antar-ārāma, antar-jyoti</i>. Commentators describe the self-sufficient sage. <i>Brahma-nirvāṇa</i> recalls 2.72.',
 'Look for the light within. It is the steadiest lamp.',
 'Joy, delight and light <b>within</b>.'),
X(25,'DELIGHTING IN ALL BEINGS’ GOOD',
 S('Sages helping people, glowing','Y','family','SARVA-BHŪTA-HITE'),
 '“The sages whose sins are destroyed, whose doubts are cut, who are self-controlled and delight in the welfare of all beings, attain the peace of Brahman.”',
 [('labhante brahma-nirvāṇam','<b>attain the peace of Brahman</b>'),('ṛṣayaḥ kṣīṇa-kalmaṣāḥ','<b>sages with sins destroyed</b>'),('chinna-dvaidhāḥ yata-ātmānaḥ','<b>doubts cut, self-controlled</b>'),('sarva-bhūta-hite ratāḥ','<b>delighting in the good of all beings</b>')],
 'Inner peace and outer service go together.',
 '<i>Sarva-bhūta-hite ratāḥ</i>, rejoicing in the good of all beings, appears again in 12.4. Commentators note the Gītā’s sage is not withdrawn but compassionate.',
 'Peace grows when we delight in others’ good.',
 'Peace and <b>love for all beings</b>.'),
X(26,'PEACE ON ALL SIDES',
 S('A sage surrounded by a glowing circle of peace','Y','light','ABHITAḤ'),
 '“For the self-controlled ascetics freed from desire and anger, whose minds are disciplined and who know the Self, the peace of Brahman is near on all sides.”',
 [('kāma-krodha-viyuktānām','<b>free from desire and anger</b>'),('yatīnām yata-cetasām','<b>of ascetics with controlled minds</b>'),('abhitaḥ brahma-nirvāṇam','<b>the peace of Brahman all around</b>'),('vartate vidita-ātmanām','<b>exists for knowers of the Self</b>')],
 'For such people, peace is all around, now and after death.',
 '<i>Abhitaḥ</i>, on both sides: here and hereafter. Commentators say the knower is surrounded by peace in every direction.',
 'Peace is not far away. It is close, when desire and anger are quiet.',
 'Peace is <b>near on all sides</b>.'),
X(27,'A FIRST GLIMPSE OF MEDITATION',
 S('A meditator looking between the eyebrows, breath even','Y','mind','BHRUVOḤ MADHYE'),
 '“Shutting out outer contacts, fixing the gaze between the eyebrows, making even the in-breath and out-breath moving within the nostrils,”',
 [('sparśān kṛtvā bahiḥ bāhyān','<b>shutting out outer contacts</b>'),('cakṣuḥ ca eva antare bhruvoḥ','<b>the gaze between the brows</b>'),('prāṇa-apānau samau kṛtvā','<b>making in- and out-breath even</b>'),('nāsā-abhyantara-cāriṇau','<b>moving within the nostrils</b>')],
 'A preview of chapter 6’s meditation.',
 'Three steps: withdraw from outer contact, focus the gaze, even the breath. Commentators link this to Patañjali’s pratyāhāra, dhāraṇā and prāṇāyāma. The verse continues into the next.',
 'Even a minute of closed eyes and even breathing can reset the mind.',
 'Close the senses, <b>even the breath</b>.'),
X(28,'EVER FREE',
 S('A meditator free of desire, fear and anger, glowing','Y','light','MUKTA'),
 '“…with senses, mind and intellect controlled, intent on freedom, free from desire, fear and anger: such a sage is free forever.”',
 [('yata-indriya-manaḥ-buddhiḥ','<b>senses, mind, intellect controlled</b>'),('muniḥ mokṣa-parāyaṇaḥ','<b>the sage intent on freedom</b>'),('vigata-icchā-bhaya-krodhaḥ','<b>free of desire, fear, anger</b>'),('yaḥ sadā muktaḥ eva saḥ','<b>he is ever free</b>')],
 'The meditator who has quieted desire, fear and anger is already free.',
 'The triad desire-fear-anger again (2.56, 4.10). Commentators: freedom is not a future reward; it is this state.',
 'Each time fear or anger fades, you taste freedom.',
 'Free of desire, fear and anger: <b>free forever</b>.'),
X(29,'KNOW ME AS FRIEND',
 S('Krishna as a loving friend of all beings, receiving offerings','K','heart','SUHṚDAM'),
 '“Knowing me as the enjoyer of sacrifices and austerities, the great Lord of all worlds, the friend of all beings, one attains peace.”',
 [('bhoktāram yajña-tapasām','<b>the enjoyer of sacrifices and austerities</b>'),('sarva-loka-maheśvaram','<b>great Lord of all worlds</b>'),('suhṛdam sarva-bhūtānām','<b>friend of all beings</b>'),('jñātvā mām śāntim ṛcchati','<b>knowing me, one attains peace</b>')],
 'The chapter ends with God as friend.',
 '<i>Suhṛt</i>, good-hearted friend. Commentators note the shift: after all the discipline, peace comes from knowing the Lord as one who loves all beings. Many devotees hold this verse dear.',
 'The Highest is not distant or harsh but a friend. That knowledge alone brings peace.',
 'The Lord is <b>the friend of all beings</b>.'),
]
